> ## What the check on 9 Oct found
>
> These are things the outline had wrong. Re-read each number before using it.
>
> - **6.2 lists the wrong feature spaces.** The similarity experiments do not use forest
>   proximity, the augmented base representations or the model's own input matrix. Those belong
>   to the noise experiments.
> - **Group exclusion.** No similarity experiment excludes a molecule's own scaffold group from
>   its neighbours. Only the similarity objective's own score does.
> - **Importance weighting is three formulas, not one.**
>   - Two experiments raise importance to the power 1, under Manhattan distance.
>   - Five use the square root, under every distance.
>   - One uses the square root scaled to sum to 1.
>   - No similarity experiment sweeps the exponent.
> - **The similarity objective is undefined on more datasets than the outline said.** Add the
>   PAM50 subtype, which has 4 classes. The code excludes eight Ginkgo sets, not five.
> - **Reversed or wider verdicts:**
>   - In the hybrid built for distance, distance does not track activity better everywhere.
>     Anti-coronavirus affinity goes 0.0901 to 0.0376 Spearman.
>   - Prediction from five neighbours at the fixed setting loses on three datasets, not one:
>     binding free energy, Buchwald-Hartwig (14.46 to 16.18 RMSE) and AC-SINS.
>   - With the setting re-picked per fold, the hybrid loses on seven datasets, not four. All
>     seven are antibody or reaction sets.
>   - On CDR flexibility, the non-cliff gain belongs to the gradient-boosted trees. LightGBM is
>     worse there too.
>   - On LogD the gain is not larger on cliff molecules.
>   - Distance in the hybrid tracks prediction error worse on six datasets, not three.
>   - Clustering flips between cluster counts on seven datasets. Retrieval loses to the
>     principal-component control on three antibody sets.
> - **Defects:**
>   - The narrower interval is not evidence about distance. The conformal interval has one
>     width per fold, so it measures the model's error in each space.
>   - Held-out molecules come out at about 40% cliff molecules, against a 15% target on the
>     training molecules.
>   - Five result files from the distance-against-activity experiment hold unweighted numbers
>     only.
>   - The kept features carry no more label information than a random subset of the same size,
>     on most datasets.
> - **What holds on small molecules alone:** the hybrid wins on all five small-molecule datasets
>   on three things:
>   - both prediction-from-neighbours comparisons;
>   - retrieval;
>   - distance tracking activity.
>
>   The exceptions are hERG for distance against error, and the QM9 cliff results, where only
>   2 of 5 folds could be scored.
> - **Missing results:** `flab_expression_koenig` has none in eight similarity experiments, and
>   QM9 was still running in three on 9 Oct. Check the newest results folder.
